@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-expectations Expectations
@brief Expectations are checks whose outcomes are counted and reported.

@details
`t.expect (condition)` checks a condition, counts the result, and reports
it; when the check fails, the test case continues, so that all its checks
are evaluated and reported in one run.

The condition is one of:
- a function comparator, such as `mt::eq (actual, 42)`, or a combination
  of them, which reports the values of its operands;
- an exception check, such as `mt::throws (...)`;
- any other value that converts to `bool` in a condition, such as
  `x == 1`, a pointer, or a `std::optional<int>`, which reports only the
  location of the failed check.

The values that would make the check pass by mistake do not compile: the
string literals and the other character arrays and pointers, the function
names, the pointers to functions and to members, and the wrappers of a
`bool` (`std::optional<bool>` and the smart pointers to `bool`), whose
condition tells whether a value is present, not what it is. The scoped
enumerations do not compile either, since only a cast converts them. Write
the comparison instead, such as `mt::eq (flag.value (), true)`. With the
deprecated definitions enabled, all of these are accepted, with a
deprecation warning, except the wrappers of a `bool` and the scoped
enumerations.

A message can be appended with `<<`, with any values that can be written,
for example `t.expect (x == 2) << "x is " << x;`. The check is reported at
the end of the statement, with the message, or, without one, with the
condition.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check the values", [] (auto& t) {
  int x = 1;
  t.expect (mt::eq (x, 2));
  t.expect (x == 2) << "x is " << x;
});
@endcode

Both checks fail; the human-readable reporter displays:

@code{.console}
    • Check the values
        ✗ FAILED (main.cpp:13, 1 == 2)
        ✗ x is 1 FAILED (main.cpp:14)
@endcode
